  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <meta
    name="description"
    content="Dash Archive — an editorial fan concept exploring Geometry Dash history, icons, and competitive players."
  />
  <title>Dash Archive — Geometry Dash fan concept</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css" />
</head>

<body>
  <header class="shell nav">
    <div class="brand">
      <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      Dash Archive
    </div>

    <nav class="links" aria-label="Main navigation">
      <a href="#history">History</a>
      <a href="#players">Players</a>
      <a href="#culture">Why it lasts</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main>
    <div class="shell hero">
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="eyebrow">A fan-made editorial concept</div>

        <h1>
          Jump.<br />
          <span class="outline">Crash.</span><br />
          Repeat.
        </h1>

        <p class="lede">
          A visual archive of the rhythm platformer that turned one
          button, a level editor, and a stubborn community into more
          than a decade of impossible-looking runs.
        </p>

        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn" href="#history">Enter the archive →</a>

          <span class="micro">
            Unofficial concept<br />
            Built for the community
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="playfield reveal"
        aria-label="Animated abstract Geometry Dash inspired playfield"
      >
        <div class="hud">
          <span>Practice mode / off</span>
          <span class="meter"><i></i></span>
        </div>

        <div class="sun"></div>
        <div class="hills"></div>

        <div class="cube">
          <div class="face"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="spike"></div>
        <div class="spike s2"></div>
        <div class="ground"></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section id="history">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-head reveal">
          <div>
            <div class="kicker">01 / The timeline</div>

            <h2>
              Built one<br />
              attempt at a time
            </h2>
          </div>

          <p>
            Geometry Dash began as a compact mobile rhythm-platformer
            and grew through official updates, user-made levels, music,
            streaming, and a famously patient creator community.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline reveal">
          <article class="era">
            <div class="year">2013</div>
            <h3>The first jump</h3>

            <p>
              Swedish developer Robert Topala releases Geometry Dash
              on mobile. Its tap-to-jump core is simple; the timing,
              memorization, and music make it relentlessly replayable.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="era">
            <div class="year">2014</div>
            <h3>Onto desktop</h3>

            <p>
              The Steam release brings the game to Windows and macOS,
              expanding the audience and making recorded completions
              and creator culture easier to share.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="era">
            <div class="year">2017</div>
            <h3>Lists &amp; legends</h3>

            <p>
              Pointercrate becomes the home of a community-run
              Demonlist, cataloguing the hardest rated levels and
              documented records at the edge of human play.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="era">
            <div class="year">2023</div>
            <h3>Update 2.2 lands</h3>

            <p>
              After years of anticipation, version 2.2 arrives with
              major editor and platforming additions—opening another
              creative era for builders and players.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="players" id="players">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-head reveal">
          <div>
            <div class="kicker">02 / Competitive standouts</div>

            <h2>
              The human<br />
              limit moves
            </h2>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is no single official “best player.” This snapshot
            uses Pointercrate’s community-run Demonlist score as one
            useful lens, alongside historical impact. Rankings change
            as new demons and records are accepted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="player-list reveal">
          <article class="player">
            <div class="rank">01</div>
            <div class="player-name">Zoink</div>

            <p>
              A defining top-level player and verifier, associated
              with landmark completions across the modern
              extreme-demon era.
            </p>

            <span class="tag">Current leader</span>
          </article>

          <article class="player">
            <div class="rank">02</div>
            <div class="player-name">wPopoff</div>

            <p>
              A leading modern competitor whose list score and elite
              completions place them among the scene’s strongest
              active players.
            </p>

            <span class="tag">Elite grinder</span>
          </article>

          <article class="player">
            <div class="rank">03</div>
            <div class="player-name">zLevii</div>

            <p>
              A high-ranking Demonlist competitor representing the
              newest wave of players pushing consistency across top
              levels.
            </p>

            <span class="tag">New generation</span>
          </article>

          <article class="player">
            <div class="rank">∞</div>
            <div class="player-name">Riot</div>

            <p>
              A historical icon whose Bloodbath verification helped
              define early extreme-demon culture and the spectacle of
              top-level play.
            </p>

            <span class="tag">Legacy icon</span>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="culture">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-head reveal">
          <div>
            <div class="kicker">03 / The loop</div>

            <h2>
              Why the game<br />
              still hits
            </h2>
          </div>

          <p>
            Geometry Dash is not only a set of official levels. It is
            a creative system: players learn, builders invent,
            musicians set the pulse, and videos turn near-impossible
            runs into shared events.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="modes">
          <article class="big-card reveal">
            <div class="card-num">01</div>

            <h3>
              Readable in a second.<br />
              Mastered in years.
            </h3>

            <p>
              One-button control keeps the game approachable, while
              speed changes, vehicles, dual modes, gravity, and custom
              triggers create enormous mechanical depth.
            </p>
          </article>

          <div class="stack">
            <article class="small-card pink reveal">
              <h3>Creator-powered</h3>

              <p>
                The level editor lets the community build its own
                visual styles, challenges, stories, and experiments.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="small-card blue reveal">
              <h3>Music as a map</h3>

              <p>
                Rhythm is feedback: beats mark danger, transitions,
                and the exact moment a practiced input becomes
                instinct.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="shell quote reveal">
      <p>
        Every impossible level is just a route
        <em>no one has memorized yet.</em>
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="shell footer-row">
      <p class="fine">
        Dash Archive is an unofficial, non-commercial fan-site concept
        and is not affiliated with RobTop Games. Player rankings are a
        dated community snapshot and should not be read as a universal
        measure of skill.
      </p>

